<LearningGuideLayout title="How to Add Custom Interactions to FPE Objects in Godot" description="Add proximity, ray-cast, or direct-input interactions to Blender-authored FPE doors, switches, pickups, and props in Godot 4." summary="FPE can carry the repeated identity and setup from Blender; your game can add the unique interaction. Doors, buttons, notes, and pickups still benefit from one small contract: can the player interact, what prompt appears, and what happens next?" relatedSlugs={["godot-trigger-system","holdable-items-godot","dialogue-triggers-godot"]} godotSource={{label:"Ray casting",href:"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/physics/ray-casting.html"}}>
    <div class="guide-summary"><strong>Use Blender commands before writing code.</strong> FPE triggers already support Enter, Exit, Interaction, Deposit, Withdraw, Hold, Release, Holdable Items Available/Unavailable, and Hold Zone Interaction. Scene Event commands can dispatch events, play or stop animations, control speakers and cameras, load levels or sub-scenes, start conversations, pause activities, delete groups, and restore player controls. Add custom Godot code only when your mechanic goes beyond that catalog.</div>
    <h2>Define a tiny interaction contract</h2><p>An interactable should answer whether it is currently usable, provide prompt text, and perform one action. Keep player input out of the door script; the player finds a target and asks it to interact.</p>
    <pre><code>class_name Interactable
extends Node3D

@export var prompt := "Use"

func can_interact(_actor: Node) -&gt; bool:
    return true

func interact(_actor: Node) -&gt; void:
    pass</code></pre>
    <h2>Three useful detection patterns</h2><h3>Proximity</h3><p>An <code>Area3D</code> tracks nearby candidates. This is forgiving and works well for cozy games, controller input, and objects that should announce themselves. Pick the closest or highest-priority candidate when several overlap.</p><h3>View-centered ray cast</h3><p>A <code>RayCast3D</code> from the camera gives precise first-person selection. Configure it to collide with the layers used by interactive physics bodies or areas, and walk from the collider to its interaction component.</p><h3>Direct pointer input</h3><p>Godot can deliver input events to a <code>CollisionObject3D</code> when object picking is enabled. This is a good fit for point-and-click scenes, but less convenient for keyboard or controller navigation.</p>
    <h2>Step-by-step implementation</h2><ol><li>Create the interaction contract and implement it on one simple switch.</li><li>Add one detector to the player and keep its current target in a single property.</li><li>Show prompt text from that current target.</li><li>Map a project input action named <code>use</code> and call the target only on the pressed edge.</li><li>Make state changes idempotent: an already-open door should not start five overlapping animations.</li></ol>
    <h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Putting bespoke ray-cast logic in every object instead of one player-side detector.</li><li>Assuming the ray collider is the scripted root; imported meshes often have collider children.</li><li>Leaving prompts visible after the target is freed or disabled.</li><li>Hard-coding key names instead of using an Input Map action.</li></ul>
    <Fragment slot="fpe"><p>Start in Blender's <strong>Trigger Events</strong> and <strong>Scene Events</strong> panels. If the available trigger types and commands can express the mechanic, you get a re-exportable artist-owned setup without custom code. For a genuinely unique mechanic, keep the imported FPE identity and add a Godot interaction component or a listener for the named event. FPE handles the repeated setup; your code owns what makes the object special.</p><p><a href="/docs/fpe-triggers-commands.html">See every FPE trigger type and Scene Event command</a>.</p></Fragment>
</LearningGuideLayout>
